1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,313
Fifty-fifty is the wrong answer to this million-dollar question.

2
00:00:04,513 --> 00:00:07,475
Three briefcases. One's got a million bucks, two are empty.

3
00:00:08,075 --> 00:00:10,742
That's a hundred bucks a day. For twenty-seven years.

4
00:00:12,042 --> 00:00:16,182
You pick a case. Then the host, who knows where the cash is, always opens an empty one.

5
00:00:16,582 --> 00:00:19,927
Now he asks: wanna switch? Tap the heart if you're thinking coin flip.

6
00:00:20,327 --> 00:00:22,217
Nah, bro. Try a hundred cases.

7
00:00:22,817 --> 00:00:25,256
You pick one, and he opens ninety-eight empties.

8
00:00:25,856 --> 00:00:29,368
He skipped just— just one case. Your pick was a one in a hundred guess.

9
00:00:29,868 --> 00:00:32,675
So the money's in his case ninety-nine percent of the time.

10
00:00:33,475 --> 00:00:35,790
Send this to the friend who'd still keep their case.

11
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:39,795
Three cases works the same. Pick the money first, and switching loses.

12
00:00:40,195 --> 00:00:44,521
Pick the first empty case, and he has to open the other empty one, so switching wins.

13
00:00:45,521 --> 00:00:47,630
And the second empty case? Same deal.

14
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:50,037
Follow, this page settles arguments.

15
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:53,026
Switching wins two times out of three.

16
00:00:54,426 --> 00:00:56,875
A columnist printed that in nineteen ninety.

17
00:00:57,275 --> 00:01:00,980
By her count, nearly a thousand PhDs wrote in to say she was wrong.

18
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:03,429
So who's wrong? Stay, or switch?
